     1	# Auto Mode
     2	
     3	Sub-document of the `orchestration` skill. Owns the **full** Auto-Mode specification: mode
     4	posture, the Always-Ask interrupt contract, the per-loop defaults (maxIterations, evaluate.mode,
     5	discuss.mode, Preparation, MEMORIZATION), the banner-conditioning note, and the maxIterations
     6	exhaustion silence contract.
     7	
     8	For the workflow governor and the global 6-step state machine, see
     9	[`orchestration/SKILL.md`](SKILL.md). For the user-driven mode that runs a per-task slice loop
    10	instead of the linear sequence, see [`orchestration/chat-mode.md`](chat-mode.md).
    11	
    12	---
    13	
    14	## §1 — Mode posture
    15	
    16	Auto Mode is the **autonomous, end-to-end orchestration mode**. The manager runs the linear
    17	6-step state machine (Configuration → Ideation → Preparation → Planning → Execution → Wrap-up)
    18	from start to finish with minimal user intervention.
    19	
    20	**Structural invariant.** Auto Mode's runtime shape is unchanged by this redesign. The 6-step
    21	state machine runs linearly; all loops run their full contract (DISCUSSION → WORK → EVALUATION →
    22	MEMORIZATION → ITER/EXIT). Nothing about the sequence, the loop structure, or the phase ordering
    23	changes in Auto Mode. This document codifies discipline that was implicit; it does not introduce
    24	new runtime behavior.
    25	
    26	**When the manager auto-proceeds.** The manager initiates each step, runs subagents, and proceeds
    27	through the loop without pausing the user for decisions in the **Auto-decide** class
    28	(see `discussion/SKILL.md § Decision Classification`). Auto-decide decisions are resolved by the
    29	codebase, project memory, rules, mistakes, or a clearly recommended approach; they are logged
    30	silently for auditability.
    31	
    32	**When the manager MUST interrupt.** The manager pauses and calls `AskUserQuestion` when:
    33	
    34	1. A decision falls in an **Always-Ask category** (Design / Scope / Destructive) — see §2.
    35	2. An eval finding implies a scope change the manager cannot resolve under existing authority.
    36	3. A step fails in a way the manager cannot resolve (e.g., `BLOCKED` status from a subagent
    37	   that has exhausted its 3-strike rule).
    38	4. The user explicitly intervenes mid-session.
    39	
    40	The manager does NOT pause for any other reason. "I'm not sure" and "this might be surprising"
    41	are not sufficient — if the decision is Auto-decide class, proceed.
    42	
    43	---
    44	
    45	## §2 — Always-Ask codification
    46	
    47	### 2.1 Authoritative source
    48	
    49	The full Always-Ask matrix lives in
    50	[`discussion/SKILL.md § Always-Ask categories (override auto-decide; the user decides)`](../discussion/SKILL.md).
    51	`auto-mode.md` references that section as the authoritative source and restates the contract in
    52	Auto-Mode-specific language so an Auto-mode manager cannot rationalize past the gate.
    53	
    54	### 2.2 Auto-Mode restatement
    55	
    56	> **In Auto Mode, the manager auto-decides everything in the Auto-decide class without pausing.
    57	> The manager MUST NOT auto-decide anything in the Always-Ask class (Design / Scope /
    58	> Destructive). For those three categories, `AskUserQuestion` fires exactly as it would in Chat
    59	> Mode — regardless of any per-step `discuss.mode: agent` setting.**
    60	
    61	The `discuss.mode: agent` default in Planning / Execution / Wrap-up (see §3) controls whether
    62	DISCUSSION rows are user-driven or agent-driven. It does **not** suppress Always-Ask interrupts.
    63	Always-Ask overrides `discuss.mode` unconditionally.
    64	
    65	### 2.3 Always-Ask categories with Auto-Mode examples
    66	
    67	| Category | Definition | Auto-Mode example |
    68	|---|---|---|
    69	| **Design** | Architecture choice, library selection, design pattern, API shape, persistence model, error-handling strategy, concurrency model. Anything that locks future code into a structural commitment. | The leader's mid-Planning research surfaces a new library not in the Ideation scope — e.g., the leader proposes `zod` for runtime schema validation when no validator was discussed in Ideation. This is a library selection (Design). The manager MUST ask before adopting it, regardless of `discuss.mode: agent`. |
    70	| **Scope** | In/out of scope of the Scope Contract, extending the contract to absorb adjacent work, narrowing to defer items mid-workflow, marking items as backlog vs in-this-workflow. | A mid-Execution executor's diff touches a file not in the plan's `files:` list — e.g., an executor editing `orchestration/SKILL.md` while scoped to `auto-mode.md` only. The manager detects the out-of-scope path and MUST ask before allowing it to proceed. |
    71	| **Destructive** | File deletion outside an explicit `files:` scope, `git reset --hard`, force-push, package downgrade, schema migration that drops data, modification of shared state outside the worktree, large-scale rename or move. | Mid-Wrap-up, an agent proposes `git reset --hard` to clean a branch after a merge conflict. This is destructive and irreversible. The manager MUST ask before issuing the command — even in Auto Mode with `discuss.mode: agent` active. |
    72	
    73	### 2.4 USER CHALLENGE cross-reference
    74	
    75	When the Planning leader's research-backed analysis substantively disagrees with the user's stated
    76	Ideation direction, the manager escalates via the USER CHALLENGE primitive in
    77	[`planning/SKILL.md § Core Principles § USER CHALLENGE`](../planning/SKILL.md). The 5-field card
    78	(What the user said / What the leader recommends / Why / What we might be missing / If we're
    79	wrong, the cost is) fires as a `AskUserQuestion` call. USER CHALLENGE is **never auto-decided**.
    80	The user's original direction is the default; the leader's recommendation only wins if the user
    81	explicitly accepts.
    82	
    83	---
    84	
    85	## §3 — Auto-Mode defaults
    86	
    87	The following defaults are locked for Auto Mode. They apply to every session that starts with
    88	`mode: "auto"` and are not overridden at the session level.
    89	
    90	| Setting | Auto default | Notes |
    91	|---|---|---|
    92	| `workflow.ideation.maxIterations` | `3` | Full exploration budget. |
    93	| `workflow.preparation.maxIterations` | `3` | Preparation runs — not skipped (contrast Chat's `0 → Skipped`). |
    94	| `workflow.planning.maxIterations` | `3` | Full planning budget. |
    95	| `workflow.execution.maxIterations` | `3` | Full execution budget. |
    96	| `workflow.wrap-up.maxIterations` | `1` | Wrap-up runs once per session. |
    97	| `evaluate.mode` (all loops) | `"always"` | Evaluation runs every loop, no mode-driven skip. `"skip"` is a power-user per-session override; the redesign does not change this, but documenting it preempts future drift. |
    98	| `workflow.ideation.discuss.mode` | `"user"` | Ideation DISCUSSION is user-driven — user confirms approach before leader works. |
    99	| `workflow.preparation.discuss.mode` | `"user"` | Preparation DISCUSSION is user-driven — user confirms readiness gaps before prep work. |
   100	| `workflow.planning.discuss.mode` | `"agent"` | Planning DISCUSSION is agent-driven — manager proceeds without a gate per loop entry. Always-Ask categories still fire (§2). |
   101	| `workflow.execution.discuss.mode` | `"agent"` | Execution DISCUSSION is agent-driven. Always-Ask categories still fire (§2). |
   102	| `workflow.wrap-up.discuss.mode` | `"agent"` | Wrap-up DISCUSSION is agent-driven. Always-Ask categories still fire (§2). |
   103	
   104	**Preparation runs.** Auto Mode does not skip Preparation. The `maxIterations: 3` value means the
   105	standard loop contract runs (DISCUSSION → WORK → EVALUATION → MEMORIZATION → ITER/EXIT). This is
   106	the structural contrast with Chat Mode's R1 lock (`maxIterations: 0 → state: Skipped`).
   107	
   108	**Full per-loop MEMORIZATION.** Auto Mode uses the **unmodified** `memorization/SKILL.md` PASS
   109	path, including Steps 6–7 (typed-finding staging). There is no "narrowed" PASS path in Auto Mode.
   110	Every loop's MEMORIZATION runs the full base procedure, including mistake-candidate staging at
   111	moment-of-capture per `mistake/SKILL.md § P2`.
   112	
   113	**Single mode question at session start.** The mode question fires once at Configuration (per
   114	PR #267 lock). The two retired setup questions stay retired.
   115	
   116	---
   117	
   118	## §4 — Banner conditioning
   119	
   120	The session-start system-reminder banner reads:
