   122	> "Auto Mode Active — bias toward working without stopping for clarifying questions."
   123	
   124	**The banner's bias is conditioned by the Always-Ask matrix (§2).** The phrase "make the
   125	reasonable call and keep going" applies to the **Auto-decide class only**. It does not extend
   126	to Always-Ask categories (Design / Scope / Destructive). A manager reading the banner's
   127	"keep going" language and using it to rationalize past an Always-Ask category is violating the
   128	Always-Ask contract, not following the banner.
   129	
   130	Operationally: when the manager faces a decision, the first question is not "should I ask?" but
   131	"which class is this?" If the decision is Auto-decide, proceed. If the decision is Always-Ask,
   132	ask — the banner is irrelevant.
   133	
   134	The banner text is injected by the harness (currently not modified by this redesign). The
   135	conditioning is a semantic note, not a code change.
   136	
   137	---
   138	
   139	## §5 — maxIterations exhaustion
   140	
   141	When a loop reaches `maxIterations` without a PASS verdict, the loop exits with `state: Aborted`
   142	and the `Aborted` verdict is stamped on the loop's record. In Auto Mode this does **not**
   143	interrupt the user mid-session. The manager notes the abort, continues to the next step (if
   144	continuing is safe), and the failure surfaces explicitly in the Wrap-up Loop's MEMORIZATION and
   145	the session handoff.
   146	
   147	This is by design — per `orchestration/SKILL.md` line 405 contract. The silence is not a bug;
   148	it is Auto Mode's autonomy contract: the user reviews outcomes at session end (Wrap-up), not
   149	mid-step. This note exists so a future reader does not mistake the mid-session silence for a
   150	missing interrupt.
   151	
   152	**Exception.** If a Planning or Execution abort makes the remaining steps unsound (e.g., Planning
   153	aborted with no deliverable plan), the manager MUST surface this via `AskUserQuestion` before
   154	proceeding to the next step — proceeding on a broken foundation is a step failure, not a
   155	recoverable abort.
