 succeeded in 115ms:
   125	## Decision Classification
   126	
   127	Every decision the manager faces falls into one of three classes. The class determines whether the manager asks the user or proceeds.
   128	
   129	### Auto-decide (proceed silently, log the decision)
   130	
   131	A decision the manager can make without user input. Resolved by the codebase, project memory, rules, mistakes, or a clearly recommended approach. The manager picks the best option, proceeds, and records the decision in the session's discussion log so it is auditable.
   132	
   133	Examples:
   134	- "Run the test suite before declaring DONE" — auto-decide; the rule is unambiguous.
   135	- "Use `bun test` vs `npm test`" — auto-decide; the codebase already uses Bun (`package.json` declares it).
   136	- "Name a new TypeScript file in camelCase vs kebab-case" — auto-decide; convention is visible in adjacent files.
   137	
   138	Auto-decide is the default for any decision that is **not** in the Always-Ask categories below.
   139	
   140	### Always-Ask categories (override auto-decide; the user decides)
   141	
   142	Three categories where the manager MUST ask the user regardless of how confident the manager feels:
   143	
   144	| Category | Examples | Why always-ask |
   145	|---|---|---|
   146	| **Design decisions** | Architecture choice, library selection, design pattern, API shape, persistence model, error-handling strategy, concurrency model. Anything that locks future code into a structural commitment. | Design is the user's domain by Ideation contract. The leader proposes; the user decides. Auto-deciding design is silent scope expansion. |
   147	| **Scope changes** | In/out of scope of the Scope Contract, extending the contract to absorb adjacent work, narrowing to defer items mid-workflow, marking items as backlog vs in-this-workflow. | The Scope Contract is user-locked in Ideation. Silent scope drift is the most common workflow failure; making scope changes always-ask makes drift impossible. |
   148	| **Destructive / irreversible operations** | File deletion (outside an explicit `files:` scope), `git reset --hard`, force-push, package downgrade, schema migration that drops data, modification of shared state outside the worktree, large-scale rename or move. | Reversibility is a quality of safe defaults; destructive operations remove the user's ability to undo. They cannot be auto-decided. |
   149	
   150	When a decision touches an Always-Ask category, the manager runs AskUserQuestion with the full question card. The user's answer is binding.
   151	
   152	### User Challenge (separate tier — already locked in `planning/SKILL.md`)
   153	
   154	When the leader's research-backed analysis substantively disagrees with the user's stated direction (typically surfaced during Planning), the manager runs a USER CHALLENGE escalation using the 5-field card defined in [`planning/SKILL.md` § Core Principles](../planning/SKILL.md#core-principles): `What the user said / What the leader recommends / Why / What we might be missing / If we're wrong, the cost is`. The user's original direction is the default — the leader's recommendation only wins if the user explicitly accepts.
   155	
   156	USER CHALLENGE is **never auto-decided**. It is the manager's tool for surfacing leader-user friction with structure.
   157	
   158	### Class assignment is documented
   159	
   160	Every AskUserQuestion call in the session's discussion log records its class — `auto-decide` (decisions made silently for auditability), `ask: design | scope | destructive` (Always-Ask category that triggered), or `user-challenge`. This makes the manager's discipline auditable after the fact.

 succeeded in 115ms:
   260	In Chat Mode, Wrap-up runs **only when the user explicitly signals end-of-session**. The signal is one of:
   261	
   262	- The user selects "Wrap up the session" at the per-task user review gate.
   263	- The user types an explicit end-of-session message (e.g., "we're done", "wrap up", "end session").
   264	- The user invokes `/gobbi wrap-up` (if and when such a command exists; otherwise the message form above).
   265	
   266	The manager does NOT auto-trigger Wrap-up on any other signal (no auto-trigger on "no more tasks for now", no auto-trigger on idle, no auto-trigger after N tasks). The discipline is symmetric to the Always-Ask Destructive category: ending the session changes durable memory (mistake promotion, archive moves, handoff write) — that decision is the user's.
   267	
   268	If the user closes the session without an explicit Wrap-up signal (terminal hang-up), the partial session-memory survives (every loop's MEMORIZATION ran, transcript + session.json + artifacts all written), and a future session can resume per the existing 3-state worktree idempotency guard (`orchestration/SKILL.md § Step 1` row 5).
